WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.830
Longevity is almost impossible in music.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.030
You know, it's hard in comedy

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.220
but I think it's almost
impossible in music.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.000
Especially popular music, so
that's gotta feel amazing.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.890
It's been a long time.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.000
Yeah.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.500
I don't know what else to compare it to.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.380
This is the only life I've
lived as far as I know,

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.973
so it seems pretty normal.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.500
But, yeah, there's not
a lot of the same bands

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.140
that we started with are still going.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.910
What do you think has helped?

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:34.267
Because the band dynamic
is so impossibly...

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.115
Um...

00:00:37.115 --> 00:00:38.215
it can be so corrosive

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:44.160
and bands usually just end up splintering,

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.200
falling apart into a million pieces.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.033
Yeah.

00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:49.230
What is it you think has
helped you guys stick it out?

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.450
First of all, we really need each other

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.540
but we're way more successful together,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.610
than any of us would be separately

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:57.450
Um-hum

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.820
and so we're kind of stuck.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.730
We're stuck together and
then just getting older

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:06.433
and, um, getting married to ...

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.783
other people. Women,
not us. Not each other

00:01:10.783 --> 00:01:11.616
(studio laughter)

00:01:11.616 --> 00:01:13.830
So you didn't all marry each other?

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:14.663
Well, I'm sorry.

00:01:15.727 --> 00:01:16.830
I read this article, my research

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.571
who's doing the fucking research here?!

00:01:18.571 --> 00:01:19.404
(studio laughter continues)

00:01:19.404 --> 00:01:20.237
Says here

00:01:20.237 --> 00:01:22.743
"and then in 1998, the band
all married each other.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.770
This unconventional right
was sanctioned by the..".

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:26.620
Okay. Well anyway

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:29.733
That: that would not have worked.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.150
I want you to think about it.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.520
What do you think it is about
you guys all getting married,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.640
which some people might think
that would pull you apart,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.280
actually helped, helped you sustain?

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.380
Because you learn how to communicate.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.220
Yeah

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.700
With, you know, in, in
your, in your marriage

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.990
you have, you have to and like, likely, um

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:52.823
yeah

00:01:52.823 --> 00:01:55.380
your partner can teach you
so much about communicating.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.230
And then in, in my case, so
I've been in couples therapy

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.260
for many years and just
learning, learning how

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.435
to communicate and then
bring that back to the band.

00:02:04.435 --> 00:02:09.270
And it now, like things
that caused so much problem

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.843
in when we're in our early
twenties in the band,

00:02:11.843 --> 00:02:15.030
and in some cases caused ruptures and

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.691
like all that stuff
would've been avoidable

00:02:17.691 --> 00:02:20.610
with better communication
that we have now.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:25.260
That's, um, I have found
that as time has gone on,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.250
I look at myself in my twenties
and thirties and I was so

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.083
driven

00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:31.860
and so ambitious

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.000
and kind of, I don't wanna, I mean

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.270
puritanical about This
is comedy and sacred

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.033
and I must be this way and
it's the only way it can be.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.700
And, and then I attributed
a lot of my mellowing to

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.910
I've become more sophisticated
and I've, you know,

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:54.810
I've become, um you know, my, my marriage

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.210
of 20 years has really
helped me and my children.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.877
And I do, you know, I, I've come up with

00:02:59.877 --> 00:03:01.200
with all these ways in
which I've become wiser.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.763
And then my wife's mom, my mother-in-law

00:03:04.763 --> 00:03:08.257
who's very smart and
a therapist, she said,

00:03:08.257 --> 00:03:09.812
"Well, your testosterone level fell."

00:03:09.812 --> 00:03:11.400
(studio laughter)

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.233
And I went, What?

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:14.858
And she went, "Testosterone
levels with men,

00:03:14.858 --> 00:03:17.850
they drop and yours, has, you
know, fallen precipitously

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.070
which is why you're so feminine now

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.149
and easy to get along with."

00:03:22.149 --> 00:03:24.150
(studio laughter)

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:25.560
And I, I was like, Oh, okay.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:26.860
Interesting. Thanks a lot.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.030
I was wearing a wonderful
dress at the time.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:33.863
Beautiful.

00:03:33.863 --> 00:03:37.710
But uh yeah, there is
something about, I, I, I love

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:38.790
um

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:43.050
we all kinda romanticize,
or it's easy to romanticize,

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:46.188
especially when you're younger,
that kind of Neil Young.

00:03:46.188 --> 00:03:49.170
It's better to, you know,
burn out than fade away and

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.330
you know, die young and
leave a good looking corpse.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.850
And as you get old you're like, fuck that.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.920
I want to be here and keep changing

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.410
and evolving and I'm
fascinated by the whole thing.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:01.649
And I'd like to keep creating
and see what I can do.

00:04:01.649 --> 00:04:04.290
And if people prefer the
earlier stuff or they

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.940
don't like what I'm doing
now, I'm gonna keep trying.

